-- THE MISSING AXIS: a type with TWO named-field CONSTRUCTORS (#1455 review, finding 1
-- ask 2).  `diff_compiler_engines` — which grades eval == native == wasm on this corpus
-- — WOULD have caught the defect that review found, because the interpreter resolves
-- fields by name at run time and is structurally immune to a wrong constant offset, so
-- it and native disagree the moment the offset is wrong.  The gate existed; the input
-- did not.  This cell is the input, and on its first CI run it immediately earned its
-- keep by red-lining a WasmGC divergence nobody had measured (see `zmulti.mdk`).
--
-- Three things are asserted, and none of them is "the new diagnostic fires":
--
--   1. A multi-named-field-constructor type is DECLARED, both constructors are
--      CONSTRUCTED, and both are consumed through `match` — the remedy the new
--      diagnostic names — with every engine agreeing on the values.  `Pair`'s
--      constructors have different arities and `Wrap`'s disagree on `alpha`'s offset,
--      so both of the shapes the guard reasons about are present and executing.
--
--   2. A type whose constructors DISAGREE (`Wrap`) may still be declared, and both of
--      its constructors constructed, without changing anything about the rest of the
--      program.  Nothing here selects `.alpha`.  That is the "feature present,
--      UNRELATED code still behaves" fixture `AGENTS.md` says a program-wide rule owes.
--
--   3. `.sx`/`.sy` on the two SINGLE-constructor records still resolve to their OWN
--      declarations, which is the property #1455 changed and the case the guard leaves
--      alone.  Their fields are at OPPOSITE offsets, so a wrong pick is a wrong VALUE
--      on the executed binary rather than an index-0 coincidence.
--
-- Expected output, DERIVED BY HAND from the sources before anything was run or captured:
--   wrapAlpha mkC = 10, wrapAlpha mkD = 110               -> w=10,110
--   pairPx mkA = 1, pairPx mkB = 3                        -> p=1,3
--   mkS is Solo { sx = 7, sy = 8 }                        -> s=7,8
--   mkO is Olos { sy = 6, sx = 5 }                        -> o=5,6
-- so `w=10,110 p=1,3 s=7,8 o=5,6`.
--
-- Counterfactual, MEASURED on a cold build of the merge base `bfcd4ea7`: an entry
-- selecting `.alpha` on a `Wrap` instead of matching printed `d=22,11` where `medaka
-- run` printed `d=11,22` (and, with the field types differing, SIGSEGV'd at exit 139) —
-- which is why that selection is not in this cell and is pinned as a rejection instead.
import zmulti.{Pair, Wrap, Solo, Olos, mkA, mkB, mkC, mkD, mkS, mkO, pairPx, wrapAlpha}

main : <IO> Unit
main =
  putStrLn
    ("w=" ++ intToString (wrapAlpha mkC) ++ "," ++ intToString (wrapAlpha mkD) ++ " p=" ++ intToString (pairPx mkA) ++ "," ++ intToString (pairPx mkB) ++ " s=" ++ intToString mkS.sx ++ "," ++ intToString mkS.sy ++ " o=" ++ intToString mkO.sx ++ "," ++ intToString mkO.sy)
